      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, if the model is trained on historical arrest data where certain neighborhoods have been disproportionately targeted, it will mathematically learn to associate those specific geographic and socioeconomic markers with higher risk. The algorithm would naturally assign a significantly higher risk score to Zone 2 because the training data reflects a history of over-policing in those areas, effectively treating poverty and density as inherent proxies for criminality. This creates a dangerous feedback loop where increased patrols generate more arrests, which then reinforces the model's belief that those zones are inherently dangerous, justifying even more resources and further entrenching the bias.",
